        </li>
      </ul><a class="textlink" href="/en/media/">Publications, media and international forums&nbsp;→</a>
    </div>
  </section><section id="applied" class="applied">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-label"><span>02</span><p>Applied work</p></div><div class="story-heading">
        <h2>Keeping power engineers in charge</h2>
      </div><div class="story-body">
        <p>
          Power engineers were responsible for keeping electricity flowing. A proposed cyber unit, staffed by outsiders,
          would change their operations while leaving them accountable. They resisted.
        </p><p>
          A new national cyber agency brought me in as a foreign expert. After studying local arrangements and
          interviewing sector professionals, I recommended keeping the engineers in charge, with cyber specialists
          alongside them.
        </p><p>
          Within weeks, the sector accepted my recommendations. Engineers helped design and review the changes, removing
          opposition and avoiding the cost of establishing and staffing a separate unit and duplicating systems.
        </p><p>
          New tools gave cyber specialists visibility across industrial control systems and IT: data they could
          correlate, use to establish normal behaviour, and monitor for anomalies—a foundation for measuring performance
          and deciding what to improve.
        </p><a class="textlink small" href="/en/media/#critical-infrastructure"
          >Related work on critical-infrastructure resilience&nbsp;→</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section><section id="research" class="research wrap">
    <div class="section-label"><span>03</span><p>Research and perspective</p></div><div class="research-grid">
      <div class="research-title">
        <h2>Innovation,<br />defense<br />and warfare</h2><a class="book" href="/en/media/#innovation"
          ><img
            class="actual-book"
            src="/images/publications/cybersecurity-in-israel.jpg"
            loading="lazy"
            alt="Cover of Cybersecurity in Israel by Lior Tabansky and Isaac Ben-Israel"
            width="827"
            height="1254"
          /><span>Cybersecurity in Israel<small>Springer, with Isaac Ben-Israel&nbsp;→</small></span></a
        >
      </div><div class="research-copy">
        <p class="lead">
          Alongside advisory work, I research <a href="/en/media/#innovation">innovation</a>, <a
            href="/en/media/#cybered-conflict">defense and warfare</a
          >.
        </p><p>
          My defense research examines why states with advanced technology and well-funded armed forces still leave
          civilian infrastructure exposed to destructive cyberattacks. I argue that AI, robotics and geopolitical
          disorder make institutional change more urgent: who is responsible, how money is spent and how people work.
        </p><a class="textlink small" href="https://en-cyber.tau.ac.il/our_team_rcrl"
          >Head, Cyber Resilience Laboratory<br />Tel Aviv University&nbsp;↗</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
